    },
    {
      "predicate": "iadas:authorConclusion",
      "object": "General sociocultural pressures (parents, peers and media) are directly associated with disordered eating, and internalization of appearance ideals and overweight preoccupation mediate these associations in female competitive athletes. Controlling for general sociocultural pressures, perceived body weight and appearance-related pressures from coaches were associated with disordered eating through appearance internationalization and overweight preoccupation."
    },
    {
      "predicate": "iadas:limites",
      "object": "Cross sectional desing; a full SEM to test the associations between latent variables was not conducted, only a basic path analysis. We did not assess variables such as negative affect and restrained eating that are integral parts of the original model. We assessed the aspects of negative body image (overweight preoccupation) and disordered eating as possible outcome of the general sociocultural and coach-related pressures."
    },
    {
      "predicate": "iadas:perspectives",
      "object": "It is important to promote resistance to sociocultural pressures (parents, peers, media) and pressures from coaches in disordered eating prevention programmes for female athletes of all ages participating in sports irrespective of sport group WS or less. Choaches must be educated to understand and recognise the associations between athletes body image, disordered eating, and physical, mental health and sports performance, as weel as their personal influence on it and to promote positive body image in sports environments."
    },
